    "fixture_id": "fx-03-back-rank-mate-positive-control",
    "description": "Verified back-rank mate in 1. Voter mate_in_n = LOW (SF mate score, no chessdb). LLM saying 'mate' or 'mate in 1' is grounded — no fire.",
    "llm_response": "This is a classic **back rank mate** pattern!\n\nLet me explain what's happening:\n\n**Current Position Analysis:**\n- Black king is on h8 (back rank)\n- Black has pawns on g7 and h7\n- White rook moves to a8\n\n**The Mate Pattern:**\nWhen White plays **Ra8+**, this creates checkmate because:\n\n1. **The rook gives check** along the 8th rank (back rank)\n2. **The black king cannot move** because:\n   - It can't go to g8 or h7 (occupied/controlled by its own pawns)\n   - It can't go to g7 (blocked by its own pawn)\n3. **The check cannot be blocked** - no piece can interpose on the 8th rank\n4. **The rook cannot be captured** - no black piece can reach a8\n\n**Key Elements of Back Rank Mate:**\n- Enemy king trapped on its back rank\n- King's escape squares blocked by its own pieces (here, the g7 and h7 pawns)\n- Attacking rook delivers check along the rank with no defense possible\n\nThis is one of the most fundamental mating patterns in chess. The black king's own pawns become a liability, creating a \"prison\" that the white rook exploits for immediate mate. Always be aware of back rank weaknesses - both to exploit them and to prevent them!",
